\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil's Economic Stabilization Yet to Be Completed -BTG's Arida\b0\par\par\uc2 944 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2012\par 20:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 18 Jul 2012 14:57 EDT *DJ  Brazil Inflation Needs to Converge Toward World Levels -Arida\par \par \uc2 18 Jul 2012 14:58 EDT *DJ  Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Regime Is a 'Dirty Float,' Says Arida\par \par \uc2 18 Jul 2012 14:58 EDT *DJ  Brazil Should Cut Interest Rates to International Levels -Arida\par \par \uc2 18 Jul 2012 15:24 EDT DJ  Brazil's Economic Stabilization Yet to Be Completed -BTG's Arida\par \par \uc2 --Brazil's stabilization yet to be completed, says BTG's Arida\par \par \uc2 --Brazil needs to reduce interest rates to international levels\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real is under a "dirty float" regime -Arida \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Luciana Magalhaes \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's economic stabilization remains incomplete, according to one of the architects of the 1994 Real Stabilization Plan, as evidenced by persistently high interest rates and a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime adding up to a "dirty float" that's not yet free from government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy suffers from a distortion, which is high interest rates," said Persio Arida in an interview. "Nominal and real interest rates have to converge toward international levels, which also implies the convergence of inflation to international levels."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While markets are not yet pricing a long-term convergence of Brazil's real interest rates toward international levels, Mr. Arida said he believes the gap will likely become considerably smaller over the years. "In this case, the market is wrong, in my opinion."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now founding partner of \f28 \uc2 \f2 BTG Pactual\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Brazil's largest independent investment bank, and chairman of its asset management arm, Mr. Arida was president of the state-run National Development Bank, or BNDES, and then during the mid-1990s he was president of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during implementation of the Real Plan, which ended a long period of high inflation in Latin America's biggest country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although down from its 2011 peak of 12.5%, Brazil's base interest rate of 8.0% is one of the highest in the world. Inflation is running at 4.9%, down from 6.5% in 2011, but still high by international standards. According to Mr. Arida, even Brazil's official 2012 inflation target of 4.5% "is higher than other countries."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 High interest rates, in turn, bring distortions to the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since they serve as a magnet for often unwanted short-term foreign investment inflows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although technically a free-floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Brazilian real really operates under a "dirty float" regime subject to frequent government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are usually aimed at keeping the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comparatively weak as a way to encourage exports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The ideal, once interest rates normalize, would be for Brazil to move from a dirty float to a pure float," Mr. Arida said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More broadly, he said Brazil should not seek to be competitive through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  controls but rather by adopting much-needed structural reforms. What needs to be done, Mr. Arida said, is to advance reforms, privatization and changes in the social security system, to eliminate distortions in the credit market, to reduce the tax burden and tariffs and barriers to the international trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even though the cycle of hyperflation ended 18 years ago, the consequences can still be felt today," Mr. Arida said. "At the time the Real Plan was launched, financial markets were atrophied and interest rates were extraordinarily high. Throughout the years, there has been a process of gradual normalization of the financial market, technically called 'financial deepening.'" But that process, he said, hasn't yet been completed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Arida praised the current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  administration, led by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Alexandre Tombini. He took exception to those who say Mr. Tombini is more tolerant of inflation than his immediate predecessor, Henrique Meirelles.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't agree with the characterization that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is more tolerant than before," said Mr. Arida. "My evaluation is that what the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has done was positive, looking at the results and at the starting point, which was an overheated economy in 2010."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After robust growth of 7.5% in 2010, Brazil's economy has been slowing down, growing just 2.7% in 2011 and is likely to expand less than 2% this year, according to economists. Besides lowering the base interest rate, Brazil has announced a series of tax cuts and spending programs to accelerate the weakening economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The best way to stimulate the economy, in the case of Brazil where the tax burden is extraordinarily high for an emerging country, is via tax cuts and not higher spending," Mr. Arida said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's tax burden, at the time of the Real Plan, was 24% of gross domestic product. Now, it stands at 37% of GDP. "But interest rates have fallen immensely since then," said Mr. Arida. "Therefore, there should be lots of room to reduce taxes. But taxes have been used to finance higher government spending and not public investment. Clearly, there are inefficiencies associated with the way the state functions in Brazil and this weighs on economic growth."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a year when economists have been proven wrong in their initial forecasts for Brazilian growth, Mr. Arida said Brazil is not alone. "The whole world has disappointed in terms of growth expectations. The only exception is Europe, because everybody already expected it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Brazil's economy is in its worst phase, but it will recover," he said, mentioning the lagging effects of the interest cuts and the fact that the country is already adopting a more expansionist fiscal policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribe to WSJ: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 18, 2012 15:24 ET (19:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120718e87i006ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
