\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ 'B' in BRIC Questionable if Brazil Doesn't Pick Up -O'Neill\b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2012\par 20:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT *DJ  Brazil Needs to Grow Faster in This Decade--Goldman's O'Neill\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT *DJ  O'Neill Expects Brazil to Grow 4.5% to 5.0% in Decade through 2020\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT *DJ  Strong Years Created False View of Brazil--O'Neill\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT *DJ  High Expectations Made It Easy For Brazil To Disappoint--O'Neill\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT *DJ  Dangerous To Assume All Brazil's Problems Stem From \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --O'Neill\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT *DJ  Brazil Government Action on \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  'To Be Welcomed' -O'Neill\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT DJ  Brazil Real Has Become Less Attractive to Investors -O'Neill\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:17 EDT *DJ  Brazil Will Need To Recover Soon After 2012--Goldman's O'Neill\par \par \uc2 11 Jul 2012 14:30 EDT DJ  'B' in BRIC Questionable if Brazil Doesn't Pick Up -O'Neill\par \par \uc2 --Creator of term "BRIC" says Brazil needs to grow faster in coming years\par \par \uc2 --What Brazil has done to the real is to be welcomed, says \uc2 Jim O'Neill\par \par \uc2 --Expectations were too high and it was easy for Brazil to disappoint\par \par \uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The "B" in "BRIC" might be called into question if Brazil's economic growth doesn't pick up in the next few years, the economist who coined the famous acronym told Dow Jones Newswires on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Jim O'Neill\uc2 , the chairman of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Asset Management, who in 2001 identified Brazil, Russia, India and China as the economic powerhouses of the future, told Dow Jones that to maintain its coveted status, Brazil ought to improve from its average growth rate of the past decade.\par \par \uc2 "What happens in 2012 in terms of GDP growth isn't so crucial," O'Neill said in a written interview. "But for the decade, Brazil will need to recover to a stronger growth rate reasonably soon after.\par \par \uc2 "If it doesn't, then the power of the 'B' in 'BRIC' would start to look a bit questionable," he added.\par \par \uc2 But despite an ongoing slowdown, Mr. O'Neill said he still expects Brazil's gross domestic product to increase by 4.5% to 5% per year on average this decade.\par \par \uc2 After a Chinese-like 7.5% growth rate in 2010, Brazil reported a timid expansion of 2.7% last year. The economy has only slowed since then, with most economists now forecasting GDP growth of around 2% in 2012 in spite of broad government efforts to spur the economy and historically low interest rates set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Mr. O'Neill said expectations for Brazil's economy may have become somewhat unrealistic, making it easy for the country to disappoint.\par \par \uc2 "People seem to forget that for much of the past 10 years, Brazil only grew by around 3.5% on average," Mr. O'Neill said. "The really strong years ... may have given people a false belief."\par \par \uc2 Outsized expectations were among the factors that attracted droves of foreign investors to Brazil in recent years. It contributed to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation that left the Brazilian real "far too strong," Mr. O'Neill said. While acknowledging that it would be dangerous to assume all of Brazil's problems were caused by a strong real, he defended the local government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures.\par \par \uc2 "I think what they have done with the real is to be welcomed," Mr. O'Neill said. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can still move. It is just that the authorities have made it less attractive for people to buy."\par \par \uc2 Over the last two years, Brazil's government has instituted a series of taxes on some short-term investment inflows, among other measures.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, O'Neill believes Brazil should tackle tough issues related to domestic competitiveness and productivity, "which require supply-side reforms and, probably, less government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  registers a 5.4 out of 10 on \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 's Growth Environment Score, a measure of 180 countries' productivity and sustainable growth. Brazil's score puts it on par with China and above both India and Russia.\par \par \uc2 "It had become the favorite of the BRIC countries," Mr. O'Neill said. "The fact that this is fading, in turn, makes it a bit easier for Brazil."\par \par \uc2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com. Paul Kiernan contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 11, 2012 14:30 ET (18:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120711e87b005ol\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
