\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Real To Keep Depreciating, Aiding Exports -Minister\b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2012\par 17:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 4 Jul 2012 11:18 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Government Has Right To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  -Mantega\par \par \uc2 4 Jul 2012 11:18 EDT *DJ  Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  War Not Over Yet -Brazil's Mantega\par \par \uc2 4 Jul 2012 11:39 EDT DJ  Brazil Real to Keep Depreciating, Aiding Exports -Minister\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega on Wednesday offered strong reinforcement to the idea that Brazil's government wishes to see a comparatively weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a way to boost exports, for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 "We have adopted the right \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to keep our exports competitive," Mantega said at a news conference. "We have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever necessary and this is now producing good results. We're going to continue with this policy so that the Brazilian real continues to depreciate, aiding Brazilian exporters."\par \par \uc2 Mantega's comments came just one day after similar remarks by Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monetary Affairs Director Aldo Mendes. On Tuesday, Mendes said Brazil's government was worried about a Brazilian real stronger than BRL2.00 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Following Mendes' comments Tuesday, the real weakened from about BRL1.98 to the dollar to BRL2.00 to the dollar. The real closed at about BRL2.01 to the dollar on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In early afternoon trading Wednesday, the real was a bit weaker than Tuesday's close at about BRL2.02 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 At Wednesday's news conference, Mantega said, "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  war I warned about long ago is not over yet. Many other countries have adopted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. We cannot be the ones excluded from this."\par \par \uc2 Mantega noted that the Brazilian real has shed about 20% of its value over the past 12 months, "leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Brazilian products, that much more competitive." He reiterated the government's forecast for a $20 billion foreign trade surplus this year. Brazil's foreign trade surplus in the first half of the year was just a little over $7 billion.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tom Murphy at tom.murphy@dowjones.com and to Rogerio Jelmayer at rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 04, 2012 11:39 ET (15:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120704e874003jx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
