\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Needs Ambitious Reforms To Spur Growth -Goldman's Paulo Leme\b0\par\par\uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2012\par 16:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 29 Jun 2012 10:59 EDT *DJ  Brazil Growth Needs Ambitious Reform -Goldman's Leme\par \par \uc2 29 Jun 2012 11:00 EDT *DJ  Brazil Economy to Grow 1.5%-2% in 2012 - Goldman's Leme\par \par \uc2 29 Jun 2012 11:00 EDT *DJ  Brazil Needs Higher Productivity, Lower Taxes -Goldman's Leme\par \par \uc2 29 Jun 2012 11:01 EDT *DJ  Weak \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Doesn't Guarantee Competitiveness - Goldman's Leme\par \par \uc2 29 Jun 2012 11:14 EDT DJ  Brazil Needs Ambitious Reforms To Spur Growth -Goldman's Paulo Leme\par \par \uc2 --Brazil will likely grow between 1.5% to 2% in 2012, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Brazil chairman\par \par \uc2 --Brazil needs to go beyond temporary measures to spur growth, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  executive\par \par \uc2 --Brazil needs higher productivity, lower taxes, education spending, says Goldman's Paulo Leme\par \par \uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil needs ambitious reforms to lift its long-term growth potential given the upheavals in the global economy, and not just measures that will likely have a temporary impact on the economy, said Paulo Leme, who in March was named chairman of Goldman Sachs \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "What we are seeing in the world isn't cyclical. It's long term, therefore, you have to think about long-term measures," Mr. Leme said in an interview in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 According to the executive, Brazil can't rely on the global economy, which will probably take very long to recover from the crisis. It must therefore look to its own internal market, and that will require considerable effort.\par \par \uc2 "The key to the safe is in the basement," Mr. Leme said.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian government has taken a series of measures to spur growth, without much success so far. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday said it expects 2.5% growth this year, while private-sector economists are increasingly looking at around 2% growth.\par \par \uc2 "I think that seeking higher growth is laudable...but, in relation to the measures taken by the government, I think they have some temporary effects. The results will depend on forces coming in the other direction," the chairman said.\par \par \uc2 Higher productivity, investments in education, lower taxes and more efficient public spending are all more concrete steps that the government should take, according to Mr. Leme. But those are all extremely difficult, both practically and politically.\par \par \uc2 "I think that they (the government) are conscious of the extent of the problem and, perhaps, they also know the political challenges that being more ambitious would bring," Mr. Leme said.\par \par \uc2 The government, Mr. Leme and many other economists have all been proven wrong this year in their expectations for economic activity, both in terms of growth and inflation. But for the executive, this isn't a surprise, given the huge problems in the global economy and the confusion that it has created.\par \par \uc2 "When you have structural fractures, the models don't respond adequately...we are not in a state of equilibrium, we are in a very dynamic situation of a financial global crisis that generates huge expectations and uncertainties," he said.\par \par \uc2 It would be no surprise if the Brazilian and the global economy again take unexpected turns, for better or for worse, in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 In December 2011, Mr. Leme forecasted Brazil's gross domestic product in 2012 would expand 3.5%. Now, the executive--who's views aren't necessarily the same as Goldman's economists -- sees the economy growing at between 1.5% to 2.0%.\par \par \uc2 The executive didn't want to make any forecasts for Brazil's exchange rate, but said it's clear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is far from being the only factor that makes Brazilian companies more competitive internationally.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government fought what it called a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," seeking to weaken the Brazilian real. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached BRL1.53 to the dollar last July and now is traded at around BRL2.02 to the dollar--following a sharp rally Friday after some positive news in Europe. The impact of the Brazilian government measures seeking to reduce the inflow of speculative capital was intensified by the risk aversion caused by the international crisis.\par \par \uc2 Despite doubts created by the continuous government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still considered an attractive country to international investors, specially the ones seeking to invest in the country for the long term, Mr. Leme said.\par \par \uc2 "The country has a lot of room to grow... but it needs to resolve its own inefficiencies," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 29, 2012 11:14 ET (15:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120629e86t004z9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
