\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Finance Minister: Growth Model Is Far From Exhausted\b0\par\par\uc2 797 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2012\par 23:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Brazil's growth model is far from exhausted, says finance minister in an interview\par \par \uc2 --Consumers, credit can continue to drive growth for years, says Guido Mantega\par \par \uc2 --Government not planning more measures to help economy, unless euro crisis worsens\par \par \uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes\par \par \uc2 Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega made a stern defense of the government's growth model, saying that the domestic market and credit can continue to grow for many years to come.\par \par \uc2 "I think it is a mistake to say that the model has been exhausted," Mr. Mantega said in an wide-ranging interview, in which he indicated that the government isn't planning more steps to boost growth, and will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to tame volatility.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the minister said, "is one of the few countries where the consumer market manages to grow."\par \par \uc2 There are still millions of Brazilians that can be lifted into the middle class and billions of dollars in investments to be made in areas such as infrastructure and logistics, Mr. Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 Consumers have become more cautious due to the global crisis, "although they do react to stimulus such as for example a reduction in prices and taxes," Mr. Mantega said. Much of the consumer debt is very short term and is paid off quickly, and Brazilians will be able to take on more debt as interest rates come down, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Credit is growing in Brazil, which shows that the theories about a depletion of credit don't hold up," he said.\par \par \uc2 There's growing criticism of the government's emphasis on domestic demand, and credit in particular, to drive the economy out of the current slump, largely a result of declining investment and industrial output--as well as the sharp drag from the global crisis. Some economists have suggested consumers are running out of steam, and that investment is in decline because the government has done little to tackle obstacles to growth in other parts of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Short-term relief has been plentiful. Tax breaks and other incentives--including new credit lines for state governments unveiled Friday--have been used to counteract the global problems, and the minister said more would only come if there is a real turn for the worse in Europe or elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 "We cannot ignore the gravity of the international crisis. The crisis has intensified," the minister said. "It hasn't improved and of course that ends up having an impact on the whole world and some effects here in Brazil."\par \par \uc2 But for now, the government seems ready to allow all the incentives to take effect.\par \par \uc2 "There's no shortage of measures which have already been taken," Mr. Mantega said. "I believe that there are now sufficient measures to provide a stimulus for the Brazilian economy."\par \par \uc2 The minister indicated he doesn't envisage any more changes to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls, and said the depreciation of the real in recent months was welcome, as it helps Brazilian companies, both in terms of competing overseas or at home. The Brazilian real is trading at BRL2.04 per dollar on Friday, compared with BRL1.70 at the end of February.\par \par \uc2 "An exchange rate at today's levels is much more comfortable than it was a year ago," Mr. Mantega said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to operate under a floating exchange rate, "so there's no ideal point," Mr. Mantega said.\par \par \uc2 "We don't have any new actions with regards to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We are only moderating fluctuations," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega said that growth will pick up in the second half of the year, in response to the steps taken already, as well as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hefty interest rate cuts. The overnight rate has fallen to a record low 8.5%, and is widely expected to be cut again in early July.\par \par \uc2 Many economists now foresee growth below 3% this year, which would be in line with last year's disappointing 2.7%.\par \par \uc2 "We think it's viable for us to have growth this year which is greater than last year," Mr. Mantega said. The minister has previously said he expects growth to be greater than 3%. "If you take the months of May and June, you already detect a recovery in various segments," he said.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the government eased some restrictions on overseas borrowing by Brazilian companies, which Mr. Mantega said had been imposed to prevent excessive borrowing rather than to interfere with the exchange rate. The government has introduced a series of other measures over the last year designed to halt or reverse the strong appreciation of the Brazilian real, which it argued was harming Brazilian companies.\par \par \uc2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com [ 06-15-12 1748ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120615e86f0015w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
