\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Poor Investor Interest Dims Brazil's Infra Bonds\b0\par\par\uc2 160 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2012\par 18:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:05 EDT - It looks less and less likely that such debt to fund roadways and tollways will be issued any time soon. Just last month, after months of planning, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  cancelled a 650 million reais infrastructure bond deal. The bank didn't say why, but market participants said the main reason was investor lack of interest in a real bond given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's almost daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policymakers have talked up these bonds as a way to attract long-term investments, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns, confusion about tax laws and lack of precedence are all constraints to any new deals hitting the market any time soon, said people familiar with the market. So, Brazil may have to look at other ways to fund its infrastructure improvements ahead of the 2014 World Cup and Olympics in 2016. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com) [ 12-06-12 1605GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1085 80000   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120612e86c000xw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
