\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Poor Investor Interest Dims Brazil's Infra Bonds\b0\par\par\uc2 1,504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2012\par 18:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:05 EDT - It looks less and less likely that such debt to fund roadways and tollways will be issued any time soon. Just last month, after months of planning, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  cancelled a 650 million reais infrastructure bond deal. The bank didn't say why, but market participants said the main reason was investor lack of interest in a real bond given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's almost daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policymakers have talked up these bonds as a way to attract long-term investments, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns, confusion about tax laws and lack of precedence are all constraints to any new deals hitting the market any time soon, said people familiar with the market. So, Brazil may have to look at other ways to fund its infrastructure improvements ahead of the 2014 World Cup and Olympics in 2016. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:03 EDT - Euro-zone woes are weighing on Canadian corporate balance sheets, the Conference Board of Canada warns, noting its leading indicator of industry profitability for May posted its biggest decline in 7 months. Of the 49 industries tracks, 13 saw drops--including oil extraction, the first for the sector since October. It's decline was linked to falling commodity prices as traders have been cutting growth prospects amid Europe's turmoil. Meanwhile, previously faster-growing sectors such as mining, gas retailing and banking posted weaker gains in May. The Conference Board's indicator tries to measure corporate profitability 3-6 months down the road. (paul.vieira@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:03 EDT - The first leg of this week's new Treasury sale at 1 pm EDT is likely go off without much fanfare. Yields are slim, but the selling pressure could lure buyers worried about the euro-zone crisis, traders say. Some traders also note cash from maturing government-guaranteed bonds sold by banks may be funneled into the 3-yr sale. Known as TLG bonds, this debt was part of the support from the US government to shore up the banking system after the 2008 financial crisis. Tom Tucci, managing director and head of Treasury trading at CIBC, says there is average of 40 billion in TLG paper rolling off on June 15, and "that money will find its way into 3-year sale." The to-be-auctioned three-year notes are recently yielding 0.379%, vs 0.362% from the May auction and 0.427% from the April sale. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:55 EDT - Most of the municipal finance professionals participating in a recent \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  survey said they expected state and local municipal debt default rates to fall this year. RBC said 56% expressed that belief at The Bond Buyer's 2012 Tri-State Area Public Finance Conference last month in New York. RBC said this represented "a significant increase in market confidence" because it was 21 points higher than the last survey, in September 2011. (mark.stein@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:51 EDT - Traders are attributing the latest leg up in US stocks to ECB Vice President Vitor Constancio's comments about a Europe-wide scheme to guarantee bank deposits. He says a at a presser that such a setup should be one element of a banking union for the euro zone. Dow industrials are up 115, about 20 points higher than before the headlines hit the wire. Whether the gains last is an open question as headlines out of Europe have often been at odds and the region's stock markets closing for the day having prompted big moves in US equities in the past. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:46 EDT - In his latest Twitter post, Bill Gross warns that German bonds could lose their safe-harbor status. Taking note of the selloff Tuesday in bunds even though Spain's 10-yr yield hit a record high, Gross tweets that "German Bunds join the maelstrom," and he sees "very few scenarios in which they do well. Only German exit favors bunds." The stance echoed Scott Mather, Pimco's head of global bond portfolio, who told Dow Jones a few weeks ago that he has been selling bunds over the past month. Mather argues that to save the euro zone, Germany will be forced to spend more taxpayer money to support Greece and other peripheral euro-zone countries. Under another scenario, Greece leaves the euro zone and an ensuing default on its euro-denominated debt subjects Germany to financial losses on the money it has plowed into the Mediterranean country over the past few years. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:45 EDT - Brazil steelmaker Usiminas' (USIM5.BR, USZNY) accord to alter its debt convenants, with a June 18 payment of a small waiver fee to bondholders, is positive for the company and its investors, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says. Company will maintain a minimum cash position of BRL3.5B and the flexibility to improve EBITDA levels for the time being, according to \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . However, cash position will tend to erode during 2013 on high investments foreseen for mining area and limited cash flow generation. This could mean the company may need to scale down its investment program and even scrap a potential bid for the construction of its own port terminal, bank says. (diana.kinch@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:40 EDT - Ongoing euro-zone turmoil has led US prime money market funds to lower their lending to banks in the euro zone by $7B in May, writes Alex Roever at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 . Lending to euro-zone banks peaked at around 32% of AUM at the end of April 2011 and has declined to about 14% of AUM as of the end of May. These conservative funds have also reduced their lending to banks that may be downgraded by \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , reallocating the funds to Treasurys and agency repo instead. They decreased their exposures to repo collateralized by non-government securities by 45B in favor of government-backed debt, increasing it by 68B. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:37 EDT - After battling to avoid completely caving in again, US stocks rip higher in what looks to be another round of furious short covering, catalyst and viability so far undetermined. Sellers were mostly having their way earlier, after an opening burst higher gave way within the first hour of trading, and several rally attempts were already short-circuited. Energy, materials sector lead the charge, followed by industrials and tech. \uc2 IBM\uc2 , \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  lead the Dow's dollar gainers. DJIA up 109; Nasdaq Comp rises 24; S&P 500 up 10. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:37 EDT - Bombardier (BBD.B.T) shares are sharply higher after winning a big Challenger business-jet order from NetJets, but Canaccord offers some words of caution. While the order's potential worth is some $7.3B based on list prices, actual prices could be 35%-plus less. The investment bank also notes it had already expected "significant Challenger deliveries in coming years," raising questions about the extent the deal will boost BBD.B.T's outlook. Lastly, Canaccord believes the contract may extend over many years, which if true, could suggest annual incremental sales "may be somewhat limited." A BBD.B.T rep wasn't immediately available for comment. Shares gain 6.6% to C$3.89, but have slumped 43% the past year. (ben.dummett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:35 EDT - Amazon (AMZN) takes another step into enemy territory, introducing its Cloud Player for iPhones and iPads through Apple's (AAPL) \uc2 App Store\uc2 . Both companies offer 5GB of free cloud storage tied to cash-minting music shops, but AMZN software isn't the default on AAPL gadgets like iTunes. That's a roadblock for any insurgent software maker trying to carve out space on competing platforms. Just ask developers at \uc2 Google\uc2  (GOOG) Maps, which AAPL is trying to replace with its own default app. While \uc2 Google\uc2  Maps has a long way to fall, AMZN's player has a steep hill to climb. (andrew.fitzgerald@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:30 EDT - \uc2 Navistar\uc2  (NAV) shares drop 6% to $27.04 after a federal appeals court vacates an EPA rule that would have allowed it to sell heavy-duty diesel truck engines that fail to meet the latest pollution standards. Competitors, which filed lawsuits challenging the EPA rule saying they had invested in technology to meet the new standards see shares climb, Cummins (CMI) up 2.1% to $94.45, and \uc2 Volvo AB\uc2  (VOLVY,VOLV-B.SK) up 1% at $11.43. Paccar (PCAR) shares also get boost after ruling, up 1.5% at $38.55. (patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 06-12-12 1205ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120612e86c000bx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
