\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexico peso hit by vote jitters, Brazil yields fall\b0\par\par\uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2012\par 16:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rise in support for leftist candidate hits Mexican peso\par \par \uc2 * No outflows seen yet, but foreign positions eyed\par \par \uc2 * Brazil yields fall on bets of further interest rate cuts\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real dips 0.1 pct, Mexico peso slumps 1.42 pct\par \par \uc2 (Rewrites throughout)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Jean Arce\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, May 31 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso slumped to a three-year low on Thursday after a poll showed a surprising surge in support for the leftist presidential candidate, while yields in Brazil fell on bets of more interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 Mexican bond prices also dropped, pushing up yields, after a newspaper poll showed Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador has cut the gap on the front-runner Enrique Pena Nieto to just four percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso has been battered this month by fears that Europe's debt troubles are deepening. But investors had been confident of a clear victory by Pena Nieto, from the centrist party that ruled Mexico for most of the 20th century.\par \par \uc2 "If other polls confirm that the gap between Lopez Obrador and Pena Nieto has closed significantly, we should expect more pressure on the exchange rate," said Benito Berber, an analyst at Nomura in New York, who was skeptical of the poll results.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think it is a correct assessment. I think it is strange that only one poll is saying this," he said.\par \par \uc2 The peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed as much as 1.5 percent to 14.3850 per dollar, its weakest level since April 2009, before pulling back to 14.37, a 1.42 percent drop.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday sold $107 million of $400 million on offer in only its second dollar auction since late 2009. The first auction was triggered last week.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso was set to clock its worst month since last September with a more than 9 percent tumble.\par \par \uc2 The sharp losses are putting pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to a free-floating peso. Mexico, perhaps the most orthodox of emerging economies, has held back from ad hoc market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , India and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 Investors in Mexican bonds have been betting that a victory by Pena Nieto could help push through stalled economic reforms and jump-start growth.\par \par \uc2 Fund managers said the poll raised concerns but added that no big foreign investors had dumped Mexican bonds yet. Traders attributed losses to local trading desks that were worried by the chance of outflows by foreigners.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have quadrupled holdings of Mexican peso debt since 2008 to around $85 billion.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the government's 10-year peso bond bid up 6 basis points to 6.14 percent. The weakness may be a buying opportunity if concerns of a Lopez Obrador victory prove unfounded.\par \par \uc2 PIMCO's Bill Gross, manager of the world's largest bond fund, said in a letter that markets might be signaling a "potential breaking point" in the world economy and that investors should buy bonds in the United States, Mexico, and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 SAMURAI BOND\par \par \uc2 Contrasting with the jitters hitting the Mexican peso and bond market, during Asian trading Mexico cracked the conservative Japanese market to sell the first unsecured Latin American debt issued in yen in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Mexico issued $80 billion yen ($1 billion) after painstakingly courting Japanese investors since last year.\par \par \uc2 "The Mexican samurai bonds drew a lot of demand because they were yen-denominated bonds," said Genzo Kimura, bond fund manager at Sumitomo Mitsui Trust Asset Management. "Japanese investors don't want to take risks when the situation in Europe looks very vulnerable."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real was little changed at 2.0164 per dollar, while Chile's peso firmed 0.19 percent to 516.40 per dollar. Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed around 6 percent during the month on concerns about the global impact of Europe's mounting credit problems.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real slumped to a three-year low last week. Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  curbed losses, and traders are eyeing the potential for more aggressive action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the real weakens past 2.05 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Yields on Brazil's interest-rate futures fell sharply after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its base Selic interest rate by half a percentage point to an all-time low of 8.5 percent late on Wednesday. 7\par \par \uc2 The decision to cut rates was unanimous, and policymakers said they still believe inflation risks remain limited, leading investors to believe another cut of half a percentage point is in the works.\par \par \uc2 The yield on interest-rate contracts maturing in January 2013 bid down 4 basis points from its adjusted close on Wednesday to 7.93 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Chikafumi Hodo in Tokyo; Editing by Padraic Cassidy)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | M | RBN | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120603e85v0007i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
