\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Closes Weaker On European Debt Concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2012\par 22:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil's real ended active trading Wednesday at BRL2.014\par \par \uc2 --Weakening of real, ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision, reflected European concerns\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday\par \par \uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real ended weaker Tuesday as concerns about the euro-zone debt crisis once again came to the forefront. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was volatile, trading around the BRL2.00 level for most of the session.\par \par \uc2 The real exited from active trading at BRL2.014 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 "A month ago, we were mainly worried about Greece. Now, we observe worsening conditions in Greece and in Spain," said Alfredo Barbutti, economist at Brazilian brokerage Liquidez.\par \par \uc2 Spain's government Wednesday denied media reports that it had submitted a plan to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to recapitalize local lender \uc2 Bankia SA\uc2  through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lending facilities.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, the Spanish government said one option to recapitalize Bankia was to issue debt securities to the bank, which it would then use as collateral to raise cash from ECB lending facilities. Government officials have said this was just one of many possibilities. ECB officials have signaled they would oppose any attempt to fund the recapitalization via the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lending facilities.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was active in the market last week, didn't hold a dollar swap auction. Barbutti said the authority probably didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the real's weaking wasn't an isolated move, as other major emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Later on Wednesday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce a decision on interest rates. The market expects Brazil's benchmark interest rate will be cut from 9% to 8.5%, the lowest level on record in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 "Even though most people are expecting a 50 basis point cut, I wouldn't be surprised if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to reduce the Selic by 25 basis points," said Mario Battistel, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Fair Brokerage. He said that given the uncertainties in overseas markets and the weakening of the real, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could decide for smaller cut.\par \par \uc2 Not all share Battistel's view. In a note to the market, a trader said on Wednesday that "with this risk background and this important move in rates globally, I think the chances of, now, an out-of-consensus cut of 75 basis points grows by the second."\par \par \uc2 A larger rate cut could affect the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market more directly, as it's likely to put off those investors that had been happy to park money and reap Brazil's juicy yields. A 0.75-percentage-point cut could lead some of them to exit positions, which could see the real weaken on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "At this point, cuts in the Selic rate especially if beyond market expectations, will have a bigger impact on [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ]," RBS said in a note.\par \par \uc2 -By Luciana Magalhaes, Dow Jones Newswires; +5511 3544-7072; luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com [ 30-05-12 2019GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120530e85u0017q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
