\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real dips as market tests  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2012\par 18:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eschews \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for second day\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso boosted by bond flows, further gains seen limited\par \par \uc2 * Brazilian real slips 0.16 pct, Mexican peso up 0.52 pct\par \par \uc2 (Rewrites throughout, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Michael O'Boyle and Danielle Fonseca\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY/SAO PAULO, May 29 (Reuters) - The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell on Tuesday as the market tested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to defend the real, while gains in other Latin American markets looked vulnerable to worries about Europe's debt troubles.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real eased to briefly touch 2 per dollar, losing ground for the first session in five after aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week drove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from a three-year low.\par \par \uc2 Global investors are eyeing the risk that Greece could leave the euro zone following elections next month, while rising borrowing costs in Spain are adding to global jitters.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained stuck in a downtrend and that Tuesday's gains in the Mexican peso likely would not last in the short term.\par \par \uc2 "There is still a lot of uncertainty," said Mauro Roca, a strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York. "If volatility does not reduce, it is going to be difficult to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trending stronger against the dollar."\par \par \uc2 Fears that Europe's debt troubles could detonate another global financial crisis hammered Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the last two months.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  TESTED\par \par \uc2 The real bid 0.16 percent weaker to 1.9859 per U.S. dollar at the close. T he \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shed as much as 1 percent earlier in the session as investors wondered whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resume \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but policymakers stayed out of the market for a second straight session.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not going to act at any cost. The weakness today was not enough to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market and the volume was not sufficient," said the head of a bank's trading desk in Brazil, who requested anonymity.\par \par \uc2 Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, a Latin America \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with RBS Securities said the market thinks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts again at the 2.05 level.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started auctioning swaps to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on May 18, when the real traded slightly above the 2 per dollar level. Po licymakers stepped up their defense as the real headed to 2.10 per greenback, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to the level of 2 per dollar on Friday.\par \par \uc2 PESO BONDS\par \par \uc2 Hopes the Chinese government may unveil new stimulus measures that would benefit Latin American exporters provided some support to the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday,\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso firmed 0.52 percent to 13.8775 per dollar, b oosted by inflows into the country's bond market, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the country's benchmark 10-year peso bond\par \par \uc2 bid down 5 basis points to 6.08 percent as its price rose.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have more than quadrupled holdings of Mexican bonds during the last three years as debt troubles in more developed economies put the shine on Mexico's healthier public finances.\par \par \uc2 Foreign buyers, who held 1.185 trillion pesos ($85 billion) in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt as of the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data on May 18, have taken advantage of the peso's recent weakness to add to their positions, traders and analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's Roca said the Mexican peso could outperform the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Europe succeeds in calming fears about a break up of the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico is closer to the United States, and the recovery in the United States should be an important driver for Latin America, barring any bad new from Europe," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico sends nearly 80 percent of its exports to the United States.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Agustin Carstens said the peso will likely rebound once European debt fears dissipate.\par \par \uc2 Analysts noted Mexican bond yields were unlikely to drive much lower in the coming weeks since they are already trading near record lows and yields could be sent higher by more global risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso was also little changed at 512.30 per dollar after closing at a 4-1/2-month low of 512.70 in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting By Michael O'Boyle; Editing by Diane Craft)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FO