\par\par \b \uc2 Reuters Summit-Former Brazil cenbankers urge economic reforms\b0\par\par\uc2 918 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2012\par 14:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For other news from Reuters Latin America Investment Summit, click on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment12"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/summit/LatinAmericanInvestment12}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 * Former cenbank chiefs Meirelles, Franco speak at summit\par \par \uc2 * See need for productivity gains, lower interest rates\par \par \uc2 * Both say Brazil is still a "buy," despite slowdown\par \par \uc2 By Asher Levine\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, May 28 (Reuters) - The outlook for Brazil's economy is still bright despite a recent slowdown, though structural reforms are needed if the country is to attain higher growth rates, according to two of the country's former central bankers.\par \par \uc2 Speaking on a panel to kick off the Reuters Latin America Investment Summit in Sao Paulo, Henrique Meirelles and Gustavo Franco argued that Brazil's consumption-driven growth model, supported over the past decade by newfound macroeconomic stability, high commodities prices and a large pool of available labor, is facing new challenges.\par \par \uc2 Both agreed that in order for Latin America's largest economy to expand more quickly, Brazil's government will have to address older challenges such as fiscal reform to keep a lid on public spending while also directing more funds to investment. That would help lower interest rates, boost investment rates, and eventually lead to higher productivity and healthier growth.\par \par \uc2 "Four percent (economic) growth is achievable," said Meirelles, who served as Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president under former President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and is currently chairman of J&F Participa\u231\'00\'E7\u245\'00\'F5es, the holding company that controls Brazilian meatpacker JBS.\par \par \uc2 "Now the name of the challenge is productivity. We are talking about infrastructure, the tax structure, education and training," he added.\par \par \uc2 Meirelles, the longest-serving \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president in Brazilian history, helped steer Brazil through its biggest economic boom in almost three decades. In 2010, the last year of his term, Brazil's economy grew 7.5 percent, as millions of Brazilians moved out of poverty and into the middle class.\par \par \uc2 That rapid growth slowed, however, to a mediocre 2.7 percent in 2011, and many analysts estimate Brazil's economy will barely surpass that figure in 2012.\par \par \uc2 In some ways, Brazil may be a victim of its own success, as record low unemployment, combined with historically scarce levels of long-term credit, make it difficult for businesses to hire workers or carry out expansion plans, said Franco, who briefly led Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  under Lula's predecessor, President Fernando Henrique Cardoso.\par \par \uc2 "There is a feeling that perhaps the model of growth is not exhausted, but tired, at least in the sense of a growth model based on low investment rates and leveraged consumption," said Franco, who currently serves as president and chief strategist of Rio Bravo Investimentos, an asset management firm.\par \par \uc2 Franco pointed to Brazil's notoriously high interest rates as a key factor limiting higher investment rates and preventing the current growth model from progressing much further.\par \par \uc2 "Interest rates are the central problem," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Its a question of creating the fundamental conditions for interest rates to be normal. Perhaps this would be the one silver bullet that could put us into 'Asian-type' investment rates," Franco said, adding that fiscal reform and a larger primary budget surplus would be key elements that could lead to those conditions.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is currently running a primary budget surplus, which excludes debt servicing costs, that is just above 3.2 percent of gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 IS BRAZIL STILL A BUY?\par \par \uc2 As the B in the BRICS group of big emerging economies, Brazil over the past decade attracted the attention of growth-seeking foreign investors. Between 2005 and 2007, more than 100 Brazilian companies went public, with foreign investors accounting for around two-thirds of the fresh funding.\par \par \uc2 This year, foreign investors flush with liquidity drove Brazilian stocks to a near 14 percent gain in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 That trend has since reversed, as concerns over Brazil's economic growth trajectory and a drop in risk appetite among global investors dragged the country's benchmark Bovespa index down over 4 percent so far in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Another factor contributing to that decline, analysts say, is the increasing unpredictability of how or when \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Investors have also been unsettled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's focus on economic growth, which in the eyes of some economists casts doubt on its commitment to inflation targeting.\par \par \uc2 Franco denied, however, that recent policy actions by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the aggressive lowering of interest rates and efforts to prevent swings in the exchange rate represent a significant shift from prior policies.\par \par \uc2 "Is it true that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil is a full clean float? Probably not," Franco said. "Is it true that we do inflation targeting without concern for growth? No. But it has always been the case. I don't think we have really changed anything fundamentally."\par \par \uc2 Despite the recent turnaround in investment flows, both Franco and Meirelles, who both spoke on Friday to Reuters, reiterated their bullishness on the Brazilian economy.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil definitely is a buy," Meirelles said, emphasizing factors such as Brazil's large domestic market, growing middle class, and stable institutional framework.\par \par \uc2 "It is a more selective buy than it was before," Meirelles said, adding that the current concerns over Brazil's economic path are both "healthy and normal" for an emerging economy.\par \par \uc2 Follow Reuters Summits on Twitter @Reuters_Summits\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Todd Benson Editing by W Simon)\par \par \uc2 LATAM-SUMMIT/BRAZIL (PIX) | ABN | E | RBN | M | D | NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120531e85s000tw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
