\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Strengthens On Greek Outlook, Exporter Dollars\b0\par\par\uc2 548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2012\par 21:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil real exits active trading at BRL1.9778 Vs BRL1.9972 Friday\par \par \uc2 --Real gains on rising hopes that Greece will remain in euro zone\par \par \uc2 --Exporters sell dollars as traders point to early-month "overshooting"\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Murphy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real ended active trading stronger against the U.S. dollar Monday as exporters sold dollars to the market following a period of what traders called "exaggerated" losses for Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real ended regular trading at BRL1.9778 to the dollar, stronger against Friday's close of BRL1.9972, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As recently as last week, the real was trading as low as BRL2.09 against the dollar, causing exporters to hold back on sales of dollars to the market in hopes of even more lucrative rates later in the month. However, persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market last week brought an appreciation of the real, convincing exporters that BRL2.00 to-the-dollar is the likely limit for any near-term depreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Rates like BRL2.04 and BRL2.05 to-the-dollar that we were seeing just a few days ago were not realistic," said Durval Correa, a trader at Sao Paulo's Multi Money brokerage. "There was a speculative element at work. That led to an exaggerated depreciation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mauro Araujo, a trader at Sao Paulo's Vision brokerage, agreed. He called last week's level a case of "overshooting." He added, "The market is beginning to sense that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is not comfortable with the real weaker than BRL2.00 to-the-dollar."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  systematically \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market last week to pull the real away from the BRL2.00 to-the-dollar level. The bank used auctions of swap contracts, sometimes at a pace of two auctions per day, to do the job. The auctions offer investors the opportunity to exchange contracts indexed to domestic interest rates for paper linked to U.S. dollars. The auctions tend to support the real against the greenback by offering investors increased opportunities to hedge in dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the market convinced that BRL2.00 is the floor for the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , exporters are now in a mood to sell their pent-up dollar holdings, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Correa said, "The market is much more likely to head in the direction of BRL1.90 to the dollar than BRL2.00." Araujo predicted a short-term "band" for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a range of BRL1.97 to BRL2.00 against the American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real was also boosted Monday by public opinion polls showing renewed support for Greece's conservative New Democracy Party, which backs the austerity policies required for that country to access a European bailout fund. The polls make it seem more likely Greece's political parties will be able to form a coalition after mid-June parliamentary elections and remain within the euro zone, thus avoiding a messy default and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restructuring.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger euro Monday helped boost global commodities prices, also a positive element for Brazil, a major commodities exporter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-07090; brazil@dowjones.com [ 28-05-12 1925GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120528e85s000sy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
