\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Brazil Real Weakens To Three-Year Low On Europe Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2012\par 21:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Brazil real exits active trading at BRL2.0589 Vs BRL2.0444 Monday\par \par \uc2 -- Real posts further losses in after-market trading at BRL2.0780\par \par \uc2 -- Real weakens despite government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via swap auctions\par \par \uc2 -- Worries over future of euro zone, Greek bailout weigh on market\par \par \uc2 (Updates with after-market trading in third graph, showing further losses for the real)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Murphy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real ended active trading weaker against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, hitting a new three-year low on continued worries about the vexing European debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real ended regular trading at BRL2.0589 to the dollar, weaker against Monday's close of BRL2.0444, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In after-market trading, the real posted additional losses, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, at BRL2.0780 against the dollar, setting the stage for possible volatility again Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened despite determined government attempts to damp volatility by means of swap contract auctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government ordered two separate auctions of swap contracts Tuesday, selling the equivalent of nearly $2.2 billion in the contracts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The auctions offer investors the opportunity to exchange paper linked to interest rates for contracts tied to the U.S. dollar, thus expanding the supply of available hedging mechanisms in the American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Increasingly, the market is seeing the level of BRL2.05 or BRL2.06 to the dollar as the trigger for government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Wanderlei Muniz, a trader at southern \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Onnix brokerage. "Both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  today [Tuesday] came as the real slipped beyond the BRL2.05 barrier."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government also \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market Friday, calling a swap auction when the real sank to BRL2.05 against the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said much of the volatility in the Brazilian market in recent days has been speculative.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "More than anything, investors are testing the government," said Olvidio Soares, a trader at Sao Paulo's Interbolsa brokerage. "They're wondering what level the government will tolerate."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Muniz noted that there was little spot trading Tuesday. "Both importers and exporters held back," he said. "They're waiting to see the outcome of the dance between speculators and the government."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the volatility also comes against the background of continued tensions in Europe, where the ever-present possibility of a messy Greek exit from the euro zone brought losses for the euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the U.S. dollar Tuesday. European leaders are due to meet later this week in efforts to find a solution not only to the Greek debt dilemma but also to wider regional problems of growth and debt-management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European crisis has left the Brazilian market in a delicate situation, according to Ilan Goldfajn, chief economist at Brazil's huge Itau (ITUB, ITUB4.BR) bank. Goldfajn said the real could weaken to BRL2.20 or even BRL2.30 against the dollar in the event of a Greek pullout from the euro zone. "But that level would be short-lived," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Muniz agreed, saying, "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can stop the depreciation of the real against the dollar any time it wants by selling dollars directly to the market." The Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holds foreign reserves of more than $370 billion, much higher than the $205 billion it held at the time of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2  crisis of 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-07090; brazil@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Rogerio Jelmayer in Sao Paulo contributed to this article [ 22-05-12 1951GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120522e85m00143\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
