\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: Brazil: Careful What You Wish For\b0\par\par\uc2 1,474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2012\par 14:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:16 (Dow Jones) "The golden smiles of ever rising return in Brazil have created a gigantic portfolio position there," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 's Sebastien Galy. "Anyone trading AUD/JPY pre and through Lehman will have a good memory of what it means." Most institutional investors have already bailed out, but "retail has not," meaning there are risks of a disorderly exit, he says. Brazil's government has long been clamoring for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but now it may be about to get what it wished for. "Without \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or in the presence of a worsening of the European crisis, the risks are looking poor," he says. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:14 (Dow Jones) The dollar is slightly lower against the yen ahead of the jobless-claims report, and it comes as US economic data continue to be a primary driver of dollar-yen. As such, a much stronger-than-expected print would likely push the dollar into positive territory against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The greenback is currently at Y80.26, versus Y80.33 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG, while the euro has rebounded some in recent minutes to get back near $1.27. (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:57 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  (WMT) comments about the alleged corruption at Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and efforts to cover it up by saying, "We are working aggressively to determine what happened, and we will take appropriate action if violations of the law or our policies occurred." Company also says it takes "compliance with the U.S. Foreign Corrupt Practices Act very seriously and continue to strengthen our anti-corruption programs around the world." The comments were contained in WMT's pre-recorded earnings call. WMT up 2.9% to $60.90. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:54 (Dow Jones) A stronger-than-expected F1Q report from \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  (DLTR) comes with a typically cool quarterly EPS forecast from the deep-discount retailer. That, a 22% stock run in 2012--and 68% the past year--as well as a 6.5% increase in per-store inventories are helping push shares down 4.7% premarket to $96.50. Many retail-sector investors haven't been too keen on companies that are boosting inventories at a faster rate than sales are growing. The negatives are outweighing things like higher operating margins last quarter, 5.6% same-store-sales growth on top of a prior-year 7.1% advance and a notable tick-up for the low end of DLTR's FY guidance. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:47 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Agilent\uc2 's (A) appears to be tapping a growing market with its decision to buy Denmark-based cancer diagnostic company Dako for $2.2B. The acquisition comes as results from more than 4,500 cancer trials were released Wednesday ahead of a key medical conference next month. One broad trend among those trials is that many newer cancer drugs are designed to target specific molecular properties of cancer tumors, in the hope they will increase effectiveness and mitigate side effects, a trend known as "personalized medicine." Patients take a diagnostic test to see if they have the genetic makeup that allows them to respond to a specific drug. Dako makes those diagnostic tests, among other things. \uc2 Agilent\uc2 's shares up 3% to $41 premarket. (george.stahl@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:38 (Dow Jones) It's \uc2 Bill Ackman\uc2  by knockout as \uc2 Canadian Pacific\uc2  (CP) CEO \uc2 Fred Green\uc2  bails, effective immediately, as it had become clear the railroad operator would lose the proxy fight at today's annual meeting. Five other company nominees, including Chairman \uc2 John Cleghorn\uc2 , say they won't stand for re-election. That leaves Ackman's 7 nominees and 9 from the company to fill the 16 slots available. Ackman has a CEO-in-waiting, saying he wanted to replace Green with former Canadian National (CNI) CEO Hunter Harrison while saying CP has lagged improvements seen by other North American railroaders. A series of investors and proxy advisers have backed the hedge-fund manager's efforts, and CP shares have climbed 80% the past 7 1/2 months. They're inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:37 (Dow Jones) Citi expects \uc2 Legg Mason\uc2  (LM) to continue to benefit from yesterday's refinancing plan that sent shares up 7.5%. Citi upgrades LM to buy from neutral as the company delevers its balance sheet, reducing risk, and shrinks its share count significantly with planned buyback. Citi says moves "should crystallize management priorities, allowing for full(er) valuation for the sizeable tax shield, which accounts for about $7 per share." Firm raises its EPS expectations and its sum of the parts target to $32.50, adding the company to its top picks list. LM up 1.2% premarket to $24.34, but remain down 28% over the last year. (patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 (Dow Jones) It's sort of an in-between day for US stocks as the latest tremor from Europe is awaited while we're 1 day out from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's debut. So what to pay attention to today? There's a host of retailers reporting quarterly results, so look for gross margins for markdown and inflation impacts and how upbeat (or not) companies are with their outlooks. There's also the dollar, which is currently at a 4-month high--coming as the euro slinks below $1.27. S&P 500 futures are down 1 while Nymex crude-oil futures are flat and gold rebounds nearly $10/troy ounce. Treasurys are little changed, with the 10-year yield at 1.76%, though Spain and Italy continue to push higher. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:24 (Dow Jones) The higher-than-expected 2.6% same-store-sales growth at \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2 's (WMT) namesake US locations highlight that the lower-income consumers that remain the base of its clientele were pretty undaunted by the still-iffy economy and higher gasoline prices last quarter. More people came in and purchase amounts rose. More is expected with 1-3% growth anticipated this quarters as gasoline prices have backed off in recent weeks. WMT is now up 3.1% premarket at $61, which equates to about 15 Dow points. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:11 (Dow Jones) Five years after Swedish private-equity fund EQT reached a $1.29B deal to buy cancer-diagnostic company Dako, it is being sold to \uc2 Agilent\uc2  (A) for $2.2B. That as the testing- and measuring-equipment maker reported decent quarterly results Monday. Cancer is a major focus for drug developers between its affliction and death rates and high price that oncology treatments typically fetch. Buying Dako is the biggest acquisition for A and comes on the heels of a host of much-smaller purchases of late. A could also use a growth driver--its shares are down 22% the past year, though have risen 14% thus far in 2012. They're inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:08 (Dow Jones) After issuing muted EPS guidance in February and it dealing with consistent margin declines, \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  (WMT) comes in with better-than-expected F1Q results for both the top and bottom lines. US same-store sales climbed 2.6% on increased purchase size and traffic levels while revenue growth remained strong overseas. WMT's 2Q EPS target brackets the Street's consensus estimate and comes as 1-3% SSS growth is projected for the namesake domestic locations after falling 0.9% a year ago, the last in a 9-quarter streak of SSS declines. WMT climbs 1.8% premarket to $60.25 after being down 1% to start 2012. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 6:46 (Dow Jones) US stock-index futures are now flat as morning losses in Europe deepen amid crisis fears there. US markets Wednesday finished right around the lowest levels of the day, so follow-through selling was a likely outcome at the outset today even without Europe's latest machinations. What could turn the tide today is weekly jobless claims at 8:30am EDT, followed by the Philly Fed manufacturing survey at 10. The Empire State reading earlier this week was stronger than anticipated. While a host of retailers are yet to report this morning, highlighted by Dow component \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  (WMT), DJIA futures are down 3 and the S&P 500 is flat. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.co \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-17-12 0816ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120517e85h00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
