\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Patience A Virtue To Switch To Long BRL\b0\par\par\uc2 1,428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2012\par 18:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:46 (Dow Jones) Patience is a virtue when waiting for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, say strategists at Citi. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real has twice clipped the BRL2.00 mark in recent days, but so far there's been no sign of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepping in to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite widespread expectations. Finance Minister Guido Mantega has played down concerns about the impact of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on inflation, "so it does not look like they are ready to show up yet. The winning strategy is usually to wait for them to show up before going long BRLCLP, BRLAUD etc," says Citi. (matthew.cowley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:46 (Dow Jones) The shape of the Treasury curve is another indicator of a shift in market sentiment toward gloom from boom. The yield spread between the benchmark 10-year note and the 2-year is at 1.51 percentage points; the narrowest Monday was 1.50, the least since January 2009. It was nearly 2 in March, but fresh euro-zone fears have sparked flight into longer-dated Treasurys, pushing the 10-year yield back toward a historic low. Analysts say further flight into long-dated Treasurys could flatten the curve more, a blow to those betting on steepening. Some investors had argued the curve should steepen to bet on higher 10-year yields once the Fed stops \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist next month. But with the euro-zone crisis back, it could raise the possibility of QE3 and further curve-flattening. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:44 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Amylin Pharmaceuticals\uc2  (AMLN) annual shareholder meeting is over and CEO \uc2 Dan Bradbury\uc2  made no mention of speculation drug maker is shopping itself around, as has been reported. Shareholders approved all items including say-on-pay advisory vote and election of directors. Bradbury talked up prospects for new diabetes drug Bydureon and metreleptin, a potential new drug for metabolic disorder in rare diseases. AMLN up 6.1% at $27.22. (peter.loftus@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:39 (Dow Jones) With some regions starting to show signs of correction and "tough steps" by the government to cool the mortgage market, "it is too early to turn to monetary policy," says \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , which notes the Canadian housing market is roughly 5-10% overvalued versus 20-30% in the US at the peak. It highlights Bank of Canada Gov. Mark Carney having said monetary policy is the last line of defense against an overheated housing market. Anyway, the investment bank contends it's impossible to direct monetary policy at one asset class while leaving other areas of the economy untouched, pointing out that Bernanke once described trying to do so as performing "brain surgery with a sledgehammer." (nirmala.menon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:34 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Energy Transfer Partners (ETP) \uc2 could fetch up to $1.8B for the 4,900 gasoline stations it acquired when it bought \uc2 Sunoco\uc2  (SUN) in April for $5.3B, says Raymond James. The big question would be whether ETP could find a buyer for the entire chain, scattered through 23 states in the Northeast, Southeast and Midwest, or would have to sell it in pieces. Possible buyers include refiner \uc2 Marathon Petroleum\uc2  (MPC), Montreal-based Alimentation \uc2 Couche-Tard\uc2  (ATD.B) and northeast retail operator \uc2 Global Partners LP\uc2  (GLP). "Energy Transfer I would believe is getting a good amount of interest," Raymond James analyst Darren Horowitz tells Dow Jones. (ben.lefebvre@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:34 (Dow Jones) \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's (JPM) sharp bounce while some momentum indicators are in oversold territory doesn't necessarily mean the rally will continue. The real price bottoms in March 2009, July 2010 and October 2011 occurred after the Relative Strength Index had first climbed back above the oversold threshold of 30, then failed to get back below it despite a lower low in price. On Aug 8, 2011, RSI bottomed at 19.40 when JPM closed at a two-year low of $33.69. When JPM bottomed on Oct 3 at a 2 1/2-year low of $28.65, RSI was at 34.55. JPM now up 3.9% at $37.19, while RSI is at 29.48. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:30 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Facebook\uc2  may still have a long way to go in terms of competing with \uc2 Google\uc2  (GOOG) in terms of the effectiveness of their online display ads, according to data released today. Online ad consulting firm Wordstream says \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's so-called clickthrough rate, or the number of times users click on an ad they see, was 0.051% in 2010, while the average for US banner ads is 0.1%. GOOG's rate stands at 0.4%, according to the data. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is gathering steam before its end-of-week IPO, and yesterday raised price range to as much as $38/share. (john.letzing@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:29 (Dow Jones) Sprint (S) CEO Dan Hesse defends the carrier's deal with Apple (AAPL) to buy at least $15.5B of iPhones the next several years, noting--among other things--that rival \uc2 T-Mobile USA\uc2  was suffering without the device. "If you have any doubt," he says during S' annual meeting, "go look at \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2 's net subscriber numbers." \uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  has lost contract users in 10 straight quarters and has said it would like to carry the iPhone, but only at the right price. Hesse says he's "very glad" to have signed the deal with AAPL, though S doesn't expected to make money from it until 2015. (greg.bensinger@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:26 (Dow Jones) Fears of an expanding euro-zone crisis haven't rattled German software giant SAP as co-CEO Bill McDermott says the company still targets 15-20% revenue growth this year. "Obviously some parts of southern Europe are under some stress, there's no doubt about that, but if you look at Germany, the UK and other parts of Europe, it's quite strong" as well as China, he says on CNBC. He even proffered: "I believe we will be profitable in the cloud in 2013," thanks largely to acquiring \uc2 SuccessFactors\uc2  last year. (steve-d.jones@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:22 (Dow Jones) Carlyle's (CG) 1Q economic earnings were down more than 26% vs year ago, but executives remind shareholders not to be too hung up about it. Noting results were "consistent with full year objectives," co-founder David Rubenstein asks investors "not to focus disproportionately on the quarter-to-quarter results. Instead, we encourage unitholders and others to take a longer term perspective." On a 12-month rolling basis, results certainly look a lot better. The 12 months ended March 31 generated $759M in distributable earnings, 36% more than the period ended March 31, 2011. CG up a fraction to $21.09. (amy.or@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:18 (Dow Jones) \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's (JPM) $2B-plus trading loss is having a "ripple effect" on other major banks, says Fitch, with CDS spreads widening on them as well. They've risen on JPM from 110 basis points Wednesday to a 4-month high of 139 this morning, according to Markit. That's no surprise, but CDS spreads on \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS) have jumped 33 to 315 while \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  (MS) and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C) see smaller increases. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  (WFC) has climbed 16bp to a 3-month high of 114. (patrick.mcgee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:14 (Dow Jones) While many investors have fled struggling Research In Motion (RIMM), \uc2 Renaissance Technologies\uc2  makes opposite bet, acquiring 7.3M RIMM shares in 1Q, ranking it as new top-10 institutional shareholder, according to Factset. Renaissance rep couldn't immediately be reached. But as a quant hedge fund manager, Renaissance's bet doesn't suggest the fund has some special insight into RIMM's fundamental prospects. Meanwhile, value manager Leon Cooperman, whose initial November disclosure of his RIMM holding created a lot of buzz, obviously got tired of waiting for a rebound. He had sold all of his 2.9M RIMM shares by the end of March. A Cooperman rep couldn't immediately be reached. (ben.dummett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.co \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-15-12 1246ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120515e85f000j8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
