\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-Brazil losing sparkle in eyes of investors\b0\par\par\uc2 972 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2012\par 21:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Refiles for technical reasons, no change to copy)\par \par \uc2 By Natalie Feary\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, May 4 (IFR) - Long the darling of Latin America, Brazil is seeing investors start to turn their attention elsewhere in the region amid a slowdown in growth and a rise in government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After boasting a 4.5% average growth rate in the five years up to 2010, the world's fifth-largest country saw growth tumble to 2.7% last year -- worse than many had expected.\par \par \uc2 And government moves to check the appreciation of Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Real, by stemming capital inflows have stoked the ire of increasingly skeptical investors.\par \par \uc2 As analysts from Nomura bluntly put it in a new report: "We believe that the era of relative outperformance by Brazil in Latin America is over."\par \par \uc2 Of course, sovereign wealth funds and other investors clearly continue to allocate substantial sums to Brazil, and Brazilian debt still accounts for more than half of corporate dollar-denominated debt out of Latin America.\par \par \uc2 But one fund manager who asked not to be named told IFR he saw anecdotal evidence that some investors were starting to withdraw money from the country.\par \par \uc2 "This makes sense," the manager said. "There is a sense of frustration among those trading in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  space."\par \par \uc2 In April there were net outflows from Brazil-dedicated fixed-income funds of USD262m, while the previous month showed a decline in inflows. Fund flows were USD3.046bn in February, down from USD4.064bn in January, according to Lipper data.\par \par \uc2 A REAL PROBLEM\par \par \uc2 The government decided earlier this year to extend the 6 percent tax on short-term foreign loans or bonds known as the IOF, in a move intended to rein in the appreciating Real.\par \par \uc2 But that move has drawn heavy fire from critics, who say different measures could and should have been taken to tighten capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of frustration regarding local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as the recent [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one New York-based investor.\par \par \uc2 "The upside of investments is capped on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 This is of particular concern because Brazil, still a developing country, has real need for more infrastructure spending.\par \par \uc2 "The infrastructure is totally inadequate and needs private-sector investment," said the fund manager.\par \par \uc2 "It used to be a case where what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did, and economists thought they should do, were the same thing. But it is no longer so. We have big concerns about the fiscal regime."\par \par \uc2 As those concerns grow, investors are taking a harder look at Brazilian debt -- and are starting to look at other countries in the region more favourably.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil has been the growth story over the last decade and still offers some opportunities, but investors are still not paying enough attention to other countries in the region," said one London-based fund manager who views Chile, Peru, Colombia and Mexico as offering more value.\par \par \uc2 In the banking sector, for instance, Jonathan Mann, head of emerging market debt at \uc2 F&C Management\uc2 , said Brazilian banks were looking expensive relative to their regional counterparts.\par \par \uc2 "When you consider where they are in the credit cycle, the slowing growth and rising delinquencies, they look expensive," he said.\par \par \uc2 Banco do Brasil\uc2 's 2020 senior bonds offer a spread of 230bp. Bancolombia's 2020 bonds offer 353bp, while Mexico's \uc2 BBVA Bancomer\uc2  2016 bonds pay a spread of 358bp.\par \par \uc2 Financial institutions outside of Brazil are popular with investors, owing to high reserve requirements as well as generally growing middle classes.\par \par \uc2 "Although spreads have widened in line with global banks, these should tighten in because [Latin American] banks are not highly leveraged like some from other regions," said Mann.\par \par \uc2 MEXICO & CO\par \par \uc2 Other sectors in Brazil look relatively even more expensive.\par \par \uc2 In the steel sector, Brazilian names are tight to where they have been trading historically and versus the comparables. The Gerdau 2021 bonds and the CSN 2020 bonds are trading with an average spread of 330bp.\par \par \uc2 ArcelorMittal\uc2  2021 bonds are trading at 400bp, \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  2021s are trading at 102bp, and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2 's 2021 bonds are trading at 125bp.\par \par \uc2 Brasil Telecom\uc2  is another name seen as fairly tight versus the comparables elsewhere. The 2022 bonds offer a yield of 5.3% and a spread of 350bp, while Deutsche Telecom's 2019 bonds offer a spread of 205bp. The spread differential has tightened 12bp on a one-year rolling average, according to one credit analyst.\par \par \uc2 "In countries like Chile, Colombia and Peru, we see value in the utility sector, particularly relative to Brazil, where we have seen some bankruptcy cases," said a New York-based investor.\par \par \uc2 "Ex-Brazil, we see value as electrical penetration is sub-optimal and not very developed. So, we would look to an active play in this sector."\par \par \uc2 Many accounts are putting their bet on Mexico -- which, unlike Brazil, is seen as having a stable and predictable fiscal and monetary regime.\par \par \uc2 Domestic consumption is expanding at 25% annually, while labour costs remain stable as productivity growth in the manufacturing sector has been averaging 2%-3%.\par \par \uc2 "Stronger growth in the US means Mexican companies should benefit and, most interestingly, the country is regaining market share from China," said a UK-based investor.\par \par \uc2 For other related fixed-income quotations, stories and guides to Reuters pages, please double click on the symbol: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  U.S. corporate bond price quotations... \tab \par  U.S. credit default swap column........ \tab \par  U.S. credit default swap news.......... \tab \par  European corporate bond market report.. \tab \par  European corporate bond market report.. \tab \par  Credit default swap guide.............. \tab \par  Fixed income guide...... \tab \par  U.S. swap spreads report............... \tab \par  U.S. Treasury market report............ \tab \par  U.S. Treasury outlook... \tab \par  U.S. municipal bond market report...... \tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Natalie Feary; Editing by Marc Carnegie)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CREDIT | ABN | D | M | NAT | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120507e854001b2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
