\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Gains in Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  to fade on dovish c.banks\b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2012\par 16:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * For poll data see\par \par \uc2 * Modest gains seen after strong start to 2012\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso, Brazil real to outperform, mainly in H2 2012\par \par \uc2 By Silvio Cascione\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, May 3 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise modestly against the dollar in coming months as local central banks seem more dovish and prone to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stimulate their local economies, a Reuters poll found.\par \par \uc2 After sharp gains in some pairs so far this year, though, traders will probably have to wait until at least the second half of the year for a fresh round of appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso and the Brazilian real are likely to perform best, particularly the real with rising inflation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaving limited room for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The shift in Latin American central banks' tones was notable, said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  analysts in a note.\par \par \uc2 "The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sounds much more dovish, as does the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Colombia, and Chile should soon follow suit," they added, noting that policymakers in the region also "have a preference for weaker exchange rates" through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But analysts remain wary of any major change to the global economic backdrop, which remains fragile.\par \par \uc2 Europe's lingering debt crisis, China's slowing economy and dwindling prospects for another round of U.S. monetary stimulus are the main risks, according to the poll of 37 economists and strategists across the region.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso is seen at 490 per dollar in a month and at 485 in a year, slightly weaker than its current level of 484 units, while the Colombian peso is forecast nearly unchanged at 1768 per dollar within one year, the poll showed.\par \par \uc2 Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strenghtened 7 percent and 9 percent this year so far, respectively, as strong capital inflows - fueled by more than 1 trillion euros in fresh money printed by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the crisis - sought fatter returns in emerging economies.\par \par \uc2 The Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped short of hiking rates and held borrowing costs for a second straight month on Monday, noting easing inflation and expressing concern about the effects of the strenghtening peso over the country's exports.\par \par \uc2 It also voted to extend for another three months its dollar purchase program. The bank will buy at least $20 million a day on the spot market until Nov. 2.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also has a record of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , cut its benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points to 5 percent in January on fears that demand in Europe and China would turn sluggish and hit the export-dependent country.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso is expected to have better fortunes than its neighbours, according to the poll, gaining through April 2013 though at a slower pace. The peso has already surged over 7 percent so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The median forecast of 16 analysts see the Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 12.73 per dollar within one year, around 2 percent stronger than Wednesday's 12.93 close.\par \par \uc2 Investors are expected to stay cautious ahead of July's presidential elections, waiting for the second half of this year to resume the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening path.\par \par \uc2 "We know its going to be a continuation of the economic model, however we don't know what are going to be the main economic policies," said Alfredo Coutino, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics director for Latin America.\par \par \uc2 REAL TO RESUME GAINS\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive push to cut interest rates to near record-lows, allied with more than $18 billion in dollar official purchases since January, has already made the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  underperform its peers.\par \par \uc2 The real is now 3 percent weaker this year -- even after jumping nearly 8 percent in the first two months of 2012.\par \par \uc2 But Brazilian policymakers may need to temper their current stance as inflation picks up in the second half of 2012, according to a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to remain nearly stable at 1.88 real per dollar by end-May, according to the median forecast of 24 estimates in the Reuters survey.\par \par \uc2 It should reverse losses from then on, trading at 1.85 real by end-July and at 1.82 per dollar by April 2013, according to median forecasts from the poll.\par \par \uc2 The Peruvian sol is expected to trade at 2.65 per dollar by end-May and at 2.61 within one year, the poll added, nearly flat from the current 2.64 rate.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Rachel Uranga and Noe Torres in Mexico City, Ursula Scollo in Lima, Nelson Bocanegra in Bogota and Moises Avila in Santiago)\par \par \uc2 LATAM-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /POLL | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | U   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120506e8530007a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngb