\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Retail Investors Find Replacements For Brazilian Real\b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2012\par 22:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the real has spurred Japanese investors to look for alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Japanese retail investors increase investments in Turkish lira and South African rand\par \par \uc2 --These investors are rotating out of Brazilian real\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Erin McCarthy \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Japanese retail investors may have finally found Brazil's replacement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's retail investor base, a powerful source of investment flows to emerging markets, has long been known for its large, bullish bets on the Brazilian real. These investments are often completed through \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -selective toshins, which are foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated investment trusts that invest in high-yielding assets like stocks and bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But with Brazil's government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  working aggressively to weaken the real, Japanese investors have pulled money out of the country. Those funds have found new homes in other emerging markets, with the Turkish lira, South African rand and Indonesian rupiah receiving more inflows, said Nomura analyst Peter Attard Montalto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've seen a very large rotation by Japanese investors away from Brazil," Montalto said, adding Brazil's efforts to weaken the real are "making people look more closely at other alternatives."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nomura data show Japanese investors purchased Y31 billion ($383 million) of toshins denominated in the Turkish lira in March, with those toshins holding JPY178 billion. They bought Y13 billion of toshins denominated in the South African rand in March, with those toshins holding Y74 billion last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These investors sold Y38.5 billion of toshins denominated in the Brazilian real in the same period. Outstanding Brazilian real toshin investments are still high, however, at Y4.716 trillion in March, according to Nomura.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's government has introduced various financial-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  taxes to curb the real's strength, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively has cut interest rates and bought dollars in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to drag down the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Firms aren't offering Brazilian-real products to Japanese investors as aggressively as they have in the past, said Koon Chow, emerging-markets strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Brazilian real still makes up the biggest chunk of assets under management in Japanese toshins, the Turkish lira is gaining in popularity, Nomura analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lira's high yields and Turkey's strong growth story are driving the movement among Japanese investors to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , analysts said. Turkey's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  maintains an interest-rate corridor of 5.75% to 11.5%, which the bank often uses for impromptu tightening to support the lira. However, Turkey's massive current-account deficit, around 10% of gross domestic product, still poses a risk for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , analysts have said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's benchmark Selic rate stands at 9% after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut it 75 basis points in April as part of an aggressive easing cycle expected to continue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Africa also boasts a relatively strong growth story, with its wealth of commodity exports serving as a main draw for investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While these investments don't have much of an impact on the spot-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since much of the exposures are hedged elsewhere, they do provide these countries with a steady source of investment flow governments are glad to see.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Japanese investors remain a force in emerging markets," said Nomura's Montalto. "Policymakers in emerging markets know that, whether it's in Turkey or South Africa."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2712; erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com; @djfxtrader [ 26-04-12 2010GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120426e84q001l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
