\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Stable Early As Market Monitors Dollar Inflows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2012\par 15:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)--Brazil's real held steady in early trading Wednesday as the market monitored for firm dollar inflows from recent corporate share sales.\par \par \uc2 As of 1310 GMT the real traded at BRL1.877 to the dollar after ending at BRL1.877 to the dollar Tuesday, according to Tullett Prebon via Factset.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was getting support in early market action after local companies tapped equities markets with public share offerings.\par \par \uc2 Among recent moves catching attention were a BRL3.6 billion initial public offering from \uc2 BTG Pactual\uc2  bank and a BRL1.4 billion offer from pulp manufacturer Fibria Cellulose (FBR). Additionally, home furnishings company Unicasa was seen tapping the market with a BRL730 million offer.\par \par \uc2 Also pushing the real stronger was a weakening of the dollar abroad, where signs of improving conditions for some European banks and governments helped lift sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Likely strong inflows of cash, meanwhile, had market participants on the lookout for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the government strives to curb strengthening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders have speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise its target range for the real to between BRL1.90 and BRL2.00 after recent market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  near the BRL1.90 level.\par \par \uc2 The market is also looking to further signals from the government on interest rates ahead of the release of the latest rate meeting minutes from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday and a possible decision from the government to alter savings account rules in a move to help bring interest rates lower.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian president Dilma Rousseff in a speech late Tuesday said the government was "calmly" evaluating the possibility that savings account rules could be changed to favor lower rates. Analysts note the government, however, may face political constraints to changing savings rules ahead of the country's mid-term elections scheduled for October.\par \par \uc2 By Gerald Jeffris; Dow Jones Newswires; +5561-9162-7863 [ 25-04-12 1329GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120425e84p0014g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
