\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 Mobius: Govt  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Might Harm Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2012\par 16:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Templeton Emerging Markets Group Executive Chairman Mark Mobius said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets might end up hurting Latin America's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 "We dislike the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the natural functioning of the markets, and it creates another variable of uncertainty that in the end goes back and affects the country's economy due to the higher risk," he said in emailed answers to questions by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Templeton Emerging Markets Group has approximately $5 billion invested in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government has been actively trying to weaken the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with measures such as higher taxes and purchases of dollars on the market, which have become even more frequent in the last few days. Deploying an "arsenal" of measures, as described by Finance Minister Guido Mantega, Brazil has been able to lead the real to a lower level than at the end of 2011, after an almost 10% gain in the first months of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Over the past two years, some emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have become overvalued on price-parity basis, creating a dilemma for policymakers as a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can make the country's exports less competitive on the global market, Mobius said.\par \par \uc2 "It's a delicate balance," he wrote. In the case of Brazil, "it would be wise if the government would make reforms to promote efficiency and innovation, rather than just fight the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 Mobius said that the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is focusing not just on defending price stability, but also, and probably first, in promoting economic activity in the country.\par \par \uc2 "As the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not independent in Brazil, this has created the view that it is working 'four hands' with the government and tolerating higher inflation."\par \par \uc2 While not formally independent, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  operates autonomously from the government.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the benchmark interest rate to 9%, from 9.75%, on Wednesday, the sixth consecutive cut by the bank's monetary-policy committee, or Copom, in the current easing cycle. Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff has been trying to spur growth, not only by weakening the real, but also by making credit cheaper and more available after a modest economic expansion of 2.7% in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Mobius said that, in the short-term, it is critical that Brazil's government addresses its spending, in size and quality terms. Over the long term, he said, Brazil should focus on increasing its productivity, especially in light of the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There should be structural reforms in areas such as education, tax, pension, the political system, privatizations and efforts to attract infrastructure investments," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mobius said he still finds attractive valuations in places such as Brazil, Russia, China and Southeast Asia, among others. When looking into investing in Brazil, he says he tends to give more emphasis to commodities and consumer companies.\par \par \uc2 "On the consumer side, we recently visited a beer and soft-drink producer in Brazil. Brazil has the potential to benefit from the increase of the minimum wage this year, and greater purchasing power is expected to increase beer consumption over cachaca (a popular Brazilian distilled alcoholic beverage)," he wrote, referring to Brazil's sugarcane liquor.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Mobius said he dislikes stocks or sectors which have strong government interference. Even though critical of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mobius said, he doesn't believe "the government wants to scare investors away as that would work against themselves, especially as the country is in the final lap to hosting major international events like the World Cup and generally seems to be behind schedule in delivering proper infrastructure."\par \par \uc2 -By Luciana Magalhaes, Dow Jones Newswires; +5511 3544-7072; luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120120420e84k001w6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
