\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Closes Weaker After  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Rate Cut\b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2012\par 21:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Real exited active trading at BRL1.8859 to the dollar, compared with BRL 1.8740 Wednesday\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut benchmark rate Wednesday, left door open for more cuts\par \par \uc2 --Real seen weakening further in coming days\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeffrey T. Lewis \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's real closed weaker Thursday, after dovish monetary policy signals from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spurred investors to re-evaluate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's likely direction in the coming weeks, and amid continuing concerns about Spain's debt situation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real exited active trading at BRL1.8859 to the dollar, after ending at BRL1.8740 Wednesday, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left markets thinking they might cut rates again at their next meeting in May," said Celso Siqueira, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Advanced brokerage in Sao Paulo. "People are worried about Spain, too, wondering if the same thing that happened with Greece can happen there."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday cut the country's reference Selic interest rate by three-quarters of a percentage point to 9%. Though the move was widely expected in markets, analysts noted the bank included some language in its statement after the meeting that suggested it could reduce the rate further than initially expected, if conditions remain favorable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants said the latest rate policy signals, combined with lingering fears regarding the persistence of a global slump, were likely to continue to weaken the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the near term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It seems likely that the real will push toward BRL1.90 to the dollar by the end of the month and perhaps move in the direction of BRL2 afterward," noted a trader at a Sao Paulo brokerage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, international factors continued to hold strong sway over the market outlook Thursday as investors mulled the prospects for improved conditions in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors' eyes were fixed on Spain, which was forced to pay higher yields in its debt auctions this week under pressure from continued fiscal difficulties. At the same time, banks such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  were evaluating the prospects that Spain, along with ailing peers Italy, Ireland and Portugal, might suffer ratings downgrades in the short term under the weight of their debts.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in the interim, is seen maintaining \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a stronger real with a continuation of daily dollar purchases in the spot market and forward auctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank has kept up dollar purchases this week, buying dollars at BRL1.87 and BRL1.88 to the dollar in separate auctions on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jeffrey T. Lewis, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 61 9278 5267; jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com [ 19-04-12 1951GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120419e84j0017j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley1