\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Brazil: No need to change savings accounts rules\b0\par\par\uc2 By Walter Brandimarte   \par \uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2012\par 23:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes from Brazil's finance minister, background)\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, April 19 (Reuters) - Brazil still doesn't need to change rules on savings accounts, one of the country's most popular investments, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its base interest rate to a near all-time low of 9 percent, Finance Minister Guido Mantega said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 As falling interest rates drive down returns in most of Brazil's bond funds, investors are encouraged to move their money into savings accounts known as "poupanca," which are tax free and pay out a fixed interest of 0.5 percent per month plus a variable rate.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday cut its base Selic rate by 75 basis points and surprised markets with hints that more cuts may follow to boost Latin America's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 If rates keep falling, the government would most likely be forced to change the rules of savings accounts - a complicated task that would require Congress' approval.\par \par \uc2 "With the base interest rate at 9 percent, we don't need to change the rules for savings accounts," Mantega told reporters in Washington after attending a meeting of the BRICS countries - Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa.\par \par \uc2 Mantega argued fixed-income funds can still remain attractive by lowering their administration fees.\par \par \uc2 "Those fees usually fall when interest rates fall," he said.\par \par \uc2 Asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Mantega defended the move as necessary to protect the national industry against competitors that are benefiting from weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We have proved that our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are efficient and can be used to mitigate the disadvantages that our industries have been facing."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting By Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Andrea Ricci)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-MANTEGA/SAVINGS  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | M | NAT | E | U | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120419e84j001g9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
