\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ S&P Sees Brazil Risks 'Balanced' At Current Ratings Level\b0\par\par\uc2 1,131 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2012\par 18:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 17 Apr 2012 12:46 EDT *DJ  S&P: Progress In Brazil Is 'Very Slow and Gradual'\par \par \uc2 17 Apr 2012 12:46 EDT *DJ  S&P: Brazil Response To Global Crisis Hard To Assess Amid Turmoil\par \par \uc2 17 Apr 2012 12:46 EDT *DJ  S&P: Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Proven Right On Decision To Cut Rates\par \par \uc2 17 Apr 2012 12:46 EDT *DJ  S&P: Hard To Assess Whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil FX Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Is Appropriate\par \par \uc2 17 Apr 2012 12:46 EDT *DJ  S&P: \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Willing To Test, Not Cross, Limits On \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 17 Apr 2012 12:47 EDT DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Progress On Growth Obstacles Is 'Slow And Gradual' -S&P\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Matthew Cowley \par   Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones) -- Brazil's taxes are high, its investment is too low, and progress in tackling the deep-rooted issues underlying those obstacles to growth is "very slow and gradual," according to analysts at ratings agency Standard and Poor's.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We see some progress in some areas, but we see the progress is very slow and gradual," said Sebastian Briozzo, S&P's director of Latin America credit. Such gradualism has been the case in Brazil over the last 15 years. For now, "the positive and the negatives on the credit are balanced at the current rating level," he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 S&P upgraded Brazil to triple-B in November, with a stable outlook, a further reflection of the country's ability to cope with international crises. Brazil's economic stability over the last decade or so has provided the government with the means to respond to global crises, helping shore up the domestic economy. But the results have been uneven, which is no surprise given the extent of the international turmoil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a world out there that's very difficult to predict," Briozzo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's economy surged sharply in 2010, sparking fears of overheating, but then dropped back quickly in 2011, as those government efforts on spending and credit were withdrawn. Economists are expecting another surge in the second half of the year as the government once again moves into action. The tools are still in the early stages of development, and it's a tricky time to be putting them to the test.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's controversial decision last August to start sharply cutting interest rates has been proven right, even though it was widely criticized by many market participants at the time, Briozzo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Brazil's growth ground to a halt late last year, amid a surging European sovereign-debt crisis, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  responded by cutting rates at a time when inflation was above the upper limit of its target and heading higher. Inflation ended the year at 6.5%; it has since dropped back to 5.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's government also took steps to prevent what it sees as an over-appreciation of the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  versus major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the dollar, arguing that the strength undermines the competitiveness of the nation's exporters and makes imports cheaper. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has bought dollars to absorb inflows and build its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, while the government has applied a financial transaction tax, known as IOF, to certain types of capital flows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "How appropriate or not this is, is difficult to assess within this environment," Briozzo said. Although the IOF taxes do introduce distortions, letting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate also has costs, Briozzo noted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government is walking a fine line between trying to deter short-term flows but attract longer-term foreign savings, which are much needed to lift investments, he said. The Brazilian government appears to be "willing to test the limits, but not to cross the limits," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In particular, an inability to increase investment is one of the major sticking points for steadier, faster economic growth, Briozzo said. Brazil's economy grew at a sluggish 2.7% last year, the slowest rate in Latin America, and its performance in recent years compares unfavorably with other emerging-market juggernauts such as India and China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a long list of reasons ... but at the end of the day we come back to fiscal issues, and there's a government that invests too little. This is the main restriction for growing at more reasonable or higher growth rates over the medium term," Briozzo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Despite all the strengths and the improvements, it's pretty difficult to get investment levels above 20%" of gross domestic product in Brazil, Briozzo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil spends about 11.5% of government revenues on interest payments, versus 7% for most triple-B-rated countries, he pointed out. "This is one of the indicators where Brazil is completely an outlier," Briozzo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With interest rates coming down, it may be an opportunity to move away from debt linked to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Selic interest rate, he said. Changes in market sentiment, and therefore on interest rates, can have a much more immediate impact in terms of driving up the government's borrowing costs when debt is linked to the Selic, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the end of February, just under 28% of federal debt was linked to the Selic, the lowest level since 1997. In a recent interview, Paulo Valle, Brazil's Treasury deputy secretary, said the government is aiming to reduce that to between 10% and 20% of total federal debt, although he didn't specify a timetable for meeting that goal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the positive side, much of Brazil's debt is now denominated in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and there's a deep pool of capital. However, "the terms of that debt is expensive, and shorter-term," Briozzo said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government's fiscal deficit last year was about 2.6% of GDP, but could fall to around 1.8% of GDP this year, as the country will be paying much less on interest, he said. Reducing interest payments is one way for the government to free up money for investments, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the analysts said the strong growth of credit in Brazil in recent years isn't a problem at the moment, but does require attention.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Is it something about to blow up? No. We're looking at it. It's an issue that requires attention and that we're following very closely because it has the potential to become a problem. So far it's not," said Roberto Sifon-Arevalo, S&P's lead analytical manager for Latin America sovereign ratings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 11 3544 7082; matthew.cowley@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 17, 2012 12:47 ET (16:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120417e84h005e5\par }\page {\par