\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 's Real Closes Weaker On  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2 , Chinese Growth\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2012\par 22:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real closed sharply weaker Friday, as concerns about China's economic slowdown and Spain's financial crisis cast a pall over global markets while the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce the supply of dollars on the local market.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL1.8370 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, compared with BRL1.8308 after the open and Thursday's close of BRL1.8260.\par \par \uc2 Dampening investor sentiment from the start of local trading was a report from China, the world's No. 2 economy and the biggest buyer of Brazilian exports, showing that gross domestic product expanded in the first quarter by its slowest pace in three years.\par \par \uc2 Other data showed a surge in borrowing by Spanish financial institutions from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reflecting weak demand from private investors and drawing attention to Spain's ongoing debt troubles. Stock markets fell broadly, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average down more than 1% at the close.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought an undisclosed amount of U.S. dollars off the local spot market in two separate auctions, after it also held two auctions Thursday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts at Citi, who on Thursday identified a "sizable inflow" of as much as $2 billion expected from a mining company, said Friday that the capital was likely to enter Brazil gradually.\par \par \uc2 "Either the supposedly hefty inflow from the mining company was offset by another substantial outflow, or its inflow will be scattered between today and April 30," Citi analyst Conrado Lima said in a note. "With higher odds of the latter option, I personally believe that the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to net the inflow smoothly."\par \par \uc2 For the upcoming week, traders have their eyes on a monetary-policy decision by Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday and data on February economic growth due out Monday.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's monetary-policy committee is widely expected to reduce its baseline Selic interest rate by 0.75 percentage point next week to 9%. With that, the current easing cycle is likely to close, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "As we have said, the international scenario should continue weakening, causing worries among monetary authorities regarding more intense contagion on the domestic economy," Santander bank said in a weekly outlook.\par \par \uc2 In other local economic news, Brazil's Planning Ministry said Friday in a budget-guideline proposal that growth should accelerate next year while inflation will remain in line with government targets. The document said the economy should grow by 5.5% in 2013 and 4.5% this year.\par \par \uc2 Planning Minister Miriam Belchior also reiterated the government's commitment to keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too much.\par \par \uc2 "We don't look at a defined parameter for the exchange rate," Belchior said. "We're going to work to avoid that it hurts the national economy, and we'll be working, as we have since 2010, to keep the rate from falling too low."\par \par \uc2 -By Paul Kiernan, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 11-3544-7074, paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 13-04-12 2050GMT ]\par \p