\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Closes Stronger Against Dollar On Europe, U.S. News\b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2012\par 22:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Real exited active trading at 1.8620 to the dollar, from BRL1.839 to the dollar Wednesday\par \par \uc2 --News from Europe and the U.S. helped strengthen the real against the dollar\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice Thursday\par \par \uc2 By Jeffrey T. Lewis\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 BRASILIA (Dow Jones)-The Brazilian real closed stronger against the dollar Thursday, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushed up by news from Europe and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at 1.8260 to the dollar, from Wednesday's close of BRL1.8329 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 "The news was good today, even Spain and Italy were able to sell their debt," said Joao Medeiros, a partner at the Pioneer brokerage in Sao Paulo. "The international news really helped the real today."\par \par \uc2 The Treasury of Italy had solid demand for its bond sales Thursday, and yields for Spanish debt fell in that country's bond auctions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. jobless figures were weaker than expected, contributing to the dollar's slide against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . New applications for jobless benefits in the U.S. rose sharply last week, another sign the labor market there might be losing momentum.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market twice Thursday, buying dollars at BRL1.8263 early in the afternoon, and then again at BRL1.8267.\par \par \uc2 The real could face strengthening pressure in coming days from a "sizable inflow" of as much as $2 billion expected from a mining company, analysts at Citibank said in a note. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to neutralize the flow, the analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, to buy dollars at BRL1.8334, and the bank's apparent readiness to continue to step in is helping to calm markets, according to Reginaldo Galhardo, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at the Treviso brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 -By Jeffrey T. Lewis, Dow Jones Newswires; +55 61 9278 5267; jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com [ 12-04-12 2022GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120412e84c00180\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
