\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Closes Weaker On Concern About Growth,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2012\par 22:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real weakened Wednesday on lingering concern about Chinese and European demand, and as investors remain wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL1.8329 to the dollar, weaker than its previous close at BRL1.8299, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 "Markets have been suffering in recent days as a result of news from Europe and China," said Eduardo Galasini, a fixed-income manager at Banco Banif in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 The real started to slump Tuesday after China reported weaker-than-expected import and export data, raising concern that Brazil's biggest trading partner could reduce its purchases of the Latin American country's commodities. In Europe, Italian and Spanish bond yields rose, reviving concern about the ability of the region to deal with high debt levels.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, yields improved somewhat, but that was countered by data from Spain showing that industrial production fell at a faster pace. That helped push up the costs of protection against a Spanish debt default.\par \par \uc2 Comments by Brazil Finance Minister Guido Mantega on Tuesday, meanwhile, affirmed the government's stance that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurts exports so the government will act to stop any gains in the real. Mantega said that the government will continue with its exchange-rate policy to avoid an appreciation of the real and to boost competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 In the past, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to buy up excess dollars in an effort to boost the dollar against the real, while the Finance Ministry has on occasion imposed higher taxes, such as the so-called IOF tax on speculative inflows.\par \par \uc2 "You have declarations from the government that they won't let the real gain [against the dollar]," Banif's Galasini said in a phone interview. "With the reduction in interest rates and the IOF there's been a reduction in spreads and so speculative investors think twice" before pouring more money into the country, he said.\par \par \uc2 Data released Wednesday showed a slight uptick in Brazil's general price index, known as the IGP-M, but still within expectations. The data did little to dissuade traders from the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut rates by 75 basis points, to 9%, when it meets next week to set the benchmark Selic rate.\par \par \uc2 Brazil has one of the highest rates in the world, which has attracted heavy inflows and worked against efforts to contain the real's strength, but each rate cut removes a bit of the appeal for traders, who borrow at near-zero rates from developed economies to buy fixed-income assets in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 At the margins there has also been an uptick in Brazilians sending money abroad as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eases restrictions. Sao Paulo-based TOV Corretora said that since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doubled in January the value of permitted transactions--raising to $100,000 from $50,000 the limit for how much Brazilian investors can freely send abroad in a single transaction--the brokerage has seen the volume of transactions go up by 40%.\par \par \uc2 TOV said in the statement that it expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to double that limit again soon, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 -By Paulo Winterstein, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7073; paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2012 16:02 ET (20:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120411e84b0068h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
