\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Seen Ending 2012 at BRL1.75, Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  - \uc2 Bradesco\uc2 's Barros\b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2012\par 17:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 5 Apr 2012 11:07 EDT *DJ  Strong Fundamentals Would Put Real At BRL1.64-BRL1.65 Without \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - Barros >BBD\par \par \uc2 5 Apr 2012 11:07 EDT *DJ  Bradesco Chief Economist: Elevated Commodities Prices Pressure Real To Appreciate >BBD\par \par \uc2 5 Apr 2012 11:07 EDT *DJ  Brazil March Inflation Shows \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Outlook Is Becoming Reality - Barros >BBD\par \par \uc2 5 Apr 2012 11:07 EDT *DJ  Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Will Likely Cut Selic Rate To 9% At Next Meeting - Barros >BBD\par \par \uc2 5 Apr 2012 11:11 EDT DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Seen Ending 2012 At BRL1.75, Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 --\uc2 Bradesco\uc2 's Barros\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real likely to end 2012 at BRL1.75 to the U.S. dollar, Bradesco chief economist says\par \par \uc2 --Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , government, real would trade at BRL1.64-BRL1.65\par \par \uc2 --Elevated commodities prices will keep appreciation pressure on real, Octavio de Barros says \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Rogerio Jelmayer and Jeff Fick \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely end 2012 at BRL1.75 to the dollar, despite continued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by the government and Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banco Bradesco\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BBD, BBDC4.BR) Chief Economist Octavio de Barros said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking on the sidelines of an event in Sao Paolo, Barros said, "looking only at the fundamentals, the real should be trading today at a rate of BRL1.64 or BRL1.65 [to the U.S. dollar]. This is based on the fact that the real is one of the so-called commodities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , such as the Australian or Canadian dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The only reason that the real is not trading at this level is because of government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Barros added.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been aggressive with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets as well as implementing measures to stave off speculative inflows in recent months. The measures have included higher taxes on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  derivatives positions, overseas loans and bond issues. The Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has stepped up spot-market dollar purchases and auctions of reverse-swap contracts, while also cutting interest rates to diminish investor interest in local fixed-income assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The forces of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation continue," Barros said, noting that the bias toward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation is largely based on commodities prices remaining at current high levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started the rate-cutting cycle in August, bringing the benchmark Selic base interest rate to 9.75% from a 2011 high of 12.5%. Brazil's interest rates, however, still remain among the highest of any major economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bradesco\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the rate reductions to continue during the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's meeting later this month, with the Selic falling to 9%, Barros said. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could also leave the door open for additional cuts, the economist added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "My outlook is still that the Selic is going to fall to 9% and be maintained at this level for a long time, but yes, there is a possibility that it could fall below this level," Barros said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The economist noted Brazil's surprisingly low inflation imprint in March, with the official IPCA consumer price index advancing 0.21% compared with February's 0.45% increase. Barros lauded the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's performance under bank President Alexandre Tombini, who deftly guided monetary policy during last year's persistent inflation fight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The IPCA surprised the whole market and proved that Tombini is enlightened," Barros said. "The scenario that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outlined is becoming reality."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rogerio Jelmayer and Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085; Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 05, 2012 11:11 ET (15:11 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120405e8450048h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
