\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Hit Sovereign Bond Issues\b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2012\par 23:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real reversed course and depreciated in March\par \par \uc2 --Government to use BRL bonds issue to keep money outside country\par \par \uc2 --The country most recently issued BRL bonds in October 2010, raising BRL1.1B\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Rogerio Jelmayer and Matthew Cowley \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's government claims success in preventing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from appreciating, but the negative collateral effects may now be spreading to the government's funding efforts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and its intention to issue bonds denominated in Brazilian reais are conflicting," said Rodrigo Fittipaldi, director of debt capital markets at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Sao Paulo. "With these signals it is difficult to create an environment of confidence for international investors [to buy real-denominated bonds]," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's government has fought to contain the strong appreciation of the Brazilian real, which it blames on easy-money policies adopted in places such as the U.S. and Europe to help resolve financial and economic crises. Brazilian officials say this makes Brazil's exports more expensive and imports cheaper, harming the local manufacturing base.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To prevent appreciation, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has bought up dollars while the government has surgically applied a financial-transactions tax, or IOF, to target specific investments deemed to be "speculative." Finance Minister Guido Mantega this week reiterated the government's willingness to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also cut interest rates aggressively, with its key rate falling to 9.75% per year, from 12.50% last July, and more cuts are expected. One of the effects of lowering rates is to reduce some of the yield over other countries, which has proved so alluring to investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a strong start to the year, the real lost ground against the dollar in March, but is still about 2% stronger from year-end, after weakening 11% against the dollar in 2011. Investors blame a conflation of various events, including renewed fears about Europe's debt problems, but also because of the Brazilian government's constant meddling, making it hard for investors to discern a broader goal or stable market outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's an impression that the government has run out of ideas," said Tony Volpon, of Nomura Securities. "There is no clear signal of what the policy is anymore."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That could now be weighing on the efforts to borrow overseas. Last week, Federal Treasury Secretary Arno Augustin said the government continues to study opportunities to launch a sovereign bond abroad denominated in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government has argued that selling real-denominated bonds overseas helps counter the appreciation trend; investors still get a juicier yield than is available on a dollar-denominated bond, but they don't have to put their money directly into Brazil. That means less pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --unless the government opts to repatriate the proceeds--and it can also attract investors that wouldn't traditionally invest in Brazilian assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We continue to insist that the risk/reward is not attractive with constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  headlines from Finance Minister Mantega, which perversely threatens \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses at a moment when public credit officials are reportedly seeking interest on [Brazilian real] global issuance," said Siobhan Morden, head of Latin America strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jefferies & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in a research report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The most recent sale of bonds denominated in Brazilian reais took place in October 2010, when the Treasury raised BRL1.1 billion by reopening a Global bond maturing in 2028, at a yield of 8.85%. One month before that, it sold a U.S. dollar bond due 2041, paying 5.2%. For some investors, that difference will continue to make denominated bonds attractive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The political and economic situation in Brazil is very stable and, even with more risks, there is a demand by international investors for [real-denominated] bonds," said Paulo Petrassi, a fund manager at Leme Investimentos in Florianopolis, Brazil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rogerio Jelmayer and Matthew Cowley, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3544-7071; rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com [ 04-04-12 2119GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1833   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120404e8440011y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
