\par\par \b \uc2 Goldman's O'Neill: Brazil Can Grow 4% Or More In Next 5 Years\b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2012\par 17:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--One of the world's top bankers painted an optimistic picture Tuesday for big emerging markets, saying Brazil and China should continue growing their economies at a healthy pace in coming years.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil can grow 4%, if not more, in the next five years," said \uc2 Jim O'Neill\uc2 , president of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Asset Management, at an event in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 O'Neill also said he sees a possible slowdown in China, the world's second-largest economy, as the result of deliberate policies by the government. But growth should continue.\par \par \uc2 "China isn't landing anytime soon," the banker said.\par \par \uc2 Burgeoning Chinese appetite for commodities such as soybeans and iron ore, of which Brazil is among the world's top producers, has provided a key pillar to Brazil's economic boom in recent years.\par \par \uc2 But economists worry about the future trajectory of Brazil's economy amid stubborn inflation and a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that hurts the industrial sector's competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian officials have become increasingly vocal in recent months about the role of excessive liquidity--a by-product of loose monetary policies in stagnating developed economies--in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation trend. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also launched wave after wave of measures and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its real.\par \par \uc2 "Policymakers are generally right to make [the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] less attractive," O'Neill said. "But I don't think they should blame everybody else in the world."\par \par \uc2 O'Neill also said Brazil's government should encourage the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act independently. The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which operates autonomously from the government, has been suspected by some analysts of coming under pressure from the government to spur growth in recent months.\par \par \uc2 -By Luciana Magalhaes and Paul Kiernan, Dow Jones Newswires; (+55)11-3544-7074, paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 04-03-12 1137ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120403e843000cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
