\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Opens Weaker As Global Economic Outlook Sours\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2012\par 14:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Economic data from abroad fail to excite global financial markets\par \par \uc2 --Concerns about sluggish growth weigh, especially prospects for Chinese slowdown\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real opens trading at BRL1.8256, weaker from Friday's close at BRL1.8227\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeff Fick \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Dow Jones)--Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened at the start of second-quarter trading Monday, undermined by economic data from abroad that continue to cast a pall over prospects for global economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weak economic data from Europe and China kept global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from making any strong moves, with the euro trading in a tight range. The real opened at BRL1.8256 to the U.S. dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. That was weaker from Friday's close at BRL1.8227. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  later weakened further to trade at BRL1.8270.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Local players have shifted attention toward prospects for global economic growth while at the same time casting a wary eye toward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. A slowing global economy would diminish demand for commodities and reduce prices, a key barometer for the real \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In China, conflicting figures on economic activity continued to raise concerns about a slowdown in the Asian giant, which is Brazil's leading trade partner and a key consumer of the country's commodities exports such as iron ore and soy. Japan's Tankan survey also showed diminished business confidence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe, meanwhile, continued to struggle under the weight of austerity measures with indicators from France, Germany and Italy showing their respective economies contracting, while the number of unemployed in the single-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc reached a record high.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The overseas troubles are also reaching Brazil, with the country's overvalued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  leading to a steep drop-off in Brazil's industrial sector. The government is expected to unveil Tuesday a new package of measures aimed at stimulating key segments of the country's industrial sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Slowing global economic momentum is pitted against awesomely accommodative monetary policy. This was a one-sided fight in [the first quarter] as investors shunned the horrifically negative real rates of return on offer from safe and sensible assets and made rude gestures in the direction of bearish pundits," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note to clients. "[The second quarter] is likely to follow a similar pattern."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Harsher discourse by Brazil's government about the strength of the real also continues to weigh on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to Miriam Tavares, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Sao Paulo trading house AGK Corretora. In her week-ahead look at markets, Tavares said that "the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate has been practically parked at a level a little above BRL1.80 to the dollar, with the tougher talk and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by authorities."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last week, the Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rolled over reverse-swap contracts maturing April 2. The bank sold more than $2 billion worth of new swaps that will mature in June and July. Reverse-swap auctions are among the measures that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  uses to support the U.S. dollar against the Brazilian real in local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. The reverse swaps remove real exposure from the market by exchanging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positions for paper linked to local interest rates, which is considered safer and less volatile.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite increasing expectations for inflation in Brazil, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also expected to continue its recent interest rate cutting cycle. Lower interest rates should diminish Brazil's allure for foreign investors seeking higher rates of return, traders said. The benchmark Selic base interest rate currently stands at 9.75%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading will likely take further direction Monday from the U.S., where the Institute for Supply Management will release manufacturing data for March and the government will report construction spending for February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-21-2586-6085; Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 02-04-12 1254GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120402e842000vp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
